<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tropaeum Alpium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7–6 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Trophy of Augustus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tropaeum Alpium, or Trophy of Augustus, is a Roman victory monument built in 7 to 6 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-turbie">La Turbie</a>, above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>, on the road through the Maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. It celebrated Augustus's conquest of the Alpine peoples, and its inscription, recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, lists 45 conquered tribes. Partly restored in the 20th century, its ruins still stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tropaeum-alpium.jpg" alt="Tropaeum Alpium" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anne Rodelato · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troph%C3%A9e_d%27Auguste_-_La_Turbie_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tropaeum Alpium commemorated the subjection of the Alpine peoples to the imperium of the Roman people under Augustus. The campaigns it recorded probably took place between 25 and 14 BCE: in 25 BCE the Salassi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a> were finally crushed, between 17 and 15 BCE peoples of the central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> were subjected and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s armies reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, and in 14 BCE the Ligurian Comati were reduced in the western Alps. The Via Julia Augusta, marked out with milestones in 13 and 12 BCE, suggests that the essential operations on the coast were over by then.</p><p>The monument stood on a spur above the col of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-turbie">La Turbie</a>, the highest point of the Via Julia Augusta as it crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Sophie Binninger notes that this low southern col was not a strategic point like the other passes, so commemorating the victory there was a symbolic choice. The trophy was novel in being dedicated to Augustus himself rather than to the god who gave victory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The text of the dedication survives only through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, who quoted it at the close of the third book of his Natural History. It states that the senate and people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> dedicated the monument to Augustus because under his command and auspices all the Alpine peoples from the upper sea to the lower, that is from the Adriatic to the Tyrrhenian, had been brought under Roman rule. It then lists the conquered peoples, among them the Trumpilini, Camunni, Vindelici, Salassi and Veragri. Pliny adds that fifteen Cottian communities which had not been hostile were not included.</p><p>The ruins show a mixed quadrangular and cylindrical building: a double square base under a circular colonnade. The architects Jean-Camille and Jules Formigé proposed that the inscription stood on the lower base, with statues of Augustus's legates and of members of his family behind the colonnade and a statue of Augustus above. Binninger stresses that this reconstruction remains largely hypothetical, as the sculpture is extremely fragmentary and there are no close parallels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The monument was repeatedly fortified and damaged over the centuries, and was partly destroyed by mining in 1705 and afterwards used as a quarry. The site was classed as a monument historique in 1865, and in 1905 to 1908 Philippe Casimir cleared it, uncovering hundreds of dressed blocks. Between 1920 and 1934 Jules Formigé rebuilt the west face to carry the inscription, using about 140 original fragments set into a modern panel, and had thirty-two houses demolished to clear the surroundings. The work was financed by the American patron Edward Tuck and inaugurated in 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tropaeum Alpium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were subdued in the Alpine campaigns of Augustus around 15 BCE and listed on the <b>Tropaeum Alpium</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ceutrones">Ceutrones</a></li><li><blockquote>The last Alpine groups were subdued under Augustus, whose Trophy at <b>La Turbie</b> (7–6 BCE) lists conquered tribes.</blockquote><a href="/state/ligures">Ligures</a></li><li><blockquote>Their name is listed among the Alpine peoples conquered by Augustus on the <b>Tropaeum Alpium</b> at La Turbie, erected in 7 to 6 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/medulli">Medulli</a></li></ul></section>
